{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"C\n    \n      Hybrid — Direct Serial Now, Router Ready\n      Connection: Implement direct serial in mavlink_interface_node (Approach A). Also add a connection_type field in hardware_params.yaml that supports tcp, serial, and udp. When you need QGC access later, drop in mavlink-routerd and switch to UDP — no code changes.\n      Camera: Same — nvarguscamerasrc via ROS2 camera node.\n      Pros: Fly and test immediately with serial. Upgrade to router when needed without touching your node code. Maximum flexibility.\n      Cons: Slightly more config parameters upfront. Need to test both serial and UDP paths.\n      ⟵ Recommended","choice":"c","id":null,"timestamp":1775064256984}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Direct Serial — Minimal Change\n      Connection: pymavlink opens /dev/ttyTHS1 directly at 921600 baud. Your mavlink_interface_node switches between TCP (sim) and serial (real) based on hardware_params.yaml.\n      Camera: Use nvarguscamerasrc via a GStreamer-based ROS2 camera node to publish to the same /camera/image topic the sim uses.\n      Pros: Fewest moving parts. No extra daemons. Lowest memory footprint (critical for 4GB).\n      Cons: Only one process can own the serial port — no QGC on a separate laptop unless you add a MAVLink router later. Single point of failure on the serial link.\n      Best for: Getting flying fast with minimum complexity.","choice":"a","id":null,"timestamp":1775064259494}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"C\n    \n      Hybrid — Direct Serial Now, Router Ready\n      Connection: Implement direct serial in mavlink_interface_node (Approach A). Also add a connection_type field in hardware_params.yaml that supports tcp, serial, and udp. When you need QGC access later, drop in mavlink-routerd and switch to UDP — no code changes.\n      Camera: Same — nvarguscamerasrc via ROS2 camera node.\n      Pros: Fly and test immediately with serial. Upgrade to router when needed without touching your node code. Maximum flexibility.\n      Cons: Slightly more config parameters upfront. Need to test both serial and UDP paths.\n      ⟵ Recommended","choice":"c","id":null,"timestamp":1775064260578}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"B\n    \n      MAVLink Router — Shared Link\n      Connection: Run mavlink-routerd on the Orin. It owns /dev/ttyTHS1 and exposes UDP endpoints. Your mavlink_interface_node connects via UDP localhost (same as it would in sim, just different address). QGC on a laptop can also connect over WiFi/Ethernet.\n      Camera: Same as A — nvarguscamerasrc via ROS2 camera node.\n      Pros: GCS operator sees live telemetry alongside autonomous operation. Familiar TCP/UDP connection for your node. Adds ~2MB RAM overhead.\n      Cons: Extra daemon to manage. Slightly more latency on MAVLink (negligible in practice). One more thing that can fail.\n      Best for: Competition day — your GCS operator needs telemetry access simultaneously.","choice":"b","id":null,"timestamp":1775064272983}
